  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"163\" data-end=\"329\">The <strong data-start=\"167\" data-end=\"187\">bicarbonate test</strong> measures the concentration of bicarbonate in the <strong data-start=\"237\" data-end=\"246\">blood</strong>, usually as part of an <strong data-start=\"270\" data-end=\"291\">electrolyte panel</strong> or <strong data-start=\"295\" data-end=\"323\">arterial blood gas (ABG)</strong> test.</p>\n<p data-start=\"331\" data-end=\"510\">Bicarbonate is a key component of the body&rsquo;s <strong data-start=\"376\" data-end=\"409\">acid-base (pH) balance system</strong>. It acts as a <strong data-start=\"424\" data-end=\"434\">buffer</strong> to maintain normal pH by neutralizing excess acids. This test helps assess:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"512\" data-end=\"853\">\n<li data-start=\"512\" data-end=\"571\">\n<p data-start=\"514\" data-end=\"571\"><strong data-start=\"514\" data-end=\"535\">Acid-base balance</strong> (metabolic acidosis or alkalosis)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"572\" data-end=\"631\">\n<p data-start=\"574\" data-end=\"631\"><strong data-start=\"574\" data-end=\"593\">Kidney function</strong> (especially in metabolic disorders)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"632\" data-end=\"696\">\n<p data-start=\"634\" data-end=\"696\"><strong data-start=\"634\" data-end=\"651\">Lung function</strong> (indirectly, via respiratory compensation)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"697\" data-end=\"775\">\n<p data-start=\"699\" data-end=\"775\">Monitoring in <strong data-start=\"713\" data-end=\"734\">chronic illnesses</strong> like COPD, kidney disease, or diabetes</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"776\" data-end=\"853\">\n<p data-start=\"778\" data-end=\"853\"><strong data-start=\"778\" data-end=\"830\">Evaluation of unexplained electrolyte imbalances</strong>, dehydration, or shock</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Possible Cause</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Metabolic alkalosis</strong></td>\n<td>Caused by vomiting, excessive diuretic use, or antacid overuse &rarr; leads to H+ loss</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Compensated respiratory acidosis</strong></td>\n<td>Lungs retain CO2 &rarr; kidneys compensate by increasing HCO3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Prolonged hypokalemia</strong></td>\n<td>Low potassium levels can lead to intracellular H+ shift, increasing serum bicarbonate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cushing&rsquo;s syndrome or mineralocorticoid excess</strong></td>\n<td>May cause retention of sodium and bicarbonate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</strong></td>\n<td>Compensatory rise in bicarbonate in response to chronic CO2 retention</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
